<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Mexican Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a>–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Mexican Empire was the monarchy established after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> won independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1821, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> crowned as Emperor Agustín I in 1822. It briefly stretched from the northern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, whose provinces were annexed in 1822. Opposition led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-lopez-de-santa-anna">Antonio López de Santa Anna</a> forced Iturbide to abdicate in 1823, and Mexico became a federal republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire.jpg" alt="First Mexican Empire" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ludovicus Ferdinandus Can have elements by Sodacan and Heralder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandera_del_Primer_Imperio_Mexicano.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Weak and divided, most of the provinces accepted annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>First Mexican Empire</b> in January 1822; San Salvador resisted until a Mexican army under Vicente Filísola occupied it in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 24 August 1821 the newly arrived Juan O’Donojú signed the Treaty of Córdoba, and in September Iturbide’s army entered Mexico City, founding the <b>First Mexican Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>San Salvador saw an early independence rising in November 1811, associated with the priest José Matías Delgado, and after independence from Spain in 1821 it resisted annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b>, its congress even voting in 1822 to seek union with the United States.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After independence from Spain in 1821 and a brief annexation to Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b>, Guatemala became the largest and most populous state of the Federal Republic of Central America, whose government sat in Guatemala City until 1834.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nicaragua emerged from the break-up of the Federal Republic of Central America, the union formed by the former provinces of the Captaincy General of Guatemala after independence from Spain in 1821 and from Agustín de Iturbide’s <b>Mexican Empire</b> in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
